## Keep moving

Staying active is one of the best things you can do. Try to be active every day, even if it is only a short walk. Low-impact activities like **swimming, cycling, walking and tai chi** are kind to joints.

## Pace yourself

Balance activity and rest. Break big jobs into smaller chunks, and change position often. Try not to overdo it on a good day, as you may pay for it afterwards.

## Managing a flare-up

- use **heat or cold** on the joint
- keep gently moving, but **do less** for a few days
- use your **pain relief** as advised
- see your GP if a flare-up does not settle

## Look after your feet

**Comfortable, supportive shoes** with a thick, soft sole can ease pain in your knees, hips and feet. Avoid high heels. Some people find insoles help. A podiatrist can advise you.

## Aids and gadgets

Equipment can make daily tasks easier and protect your joints, for example:

- a **walking stick**, used in the hand opposite the painful hip or knee
- **jar openers, tap turners** and **easy-grip cutlery** for sore hands
- a **raised toilet seat**, **grab rails** and a **perching stool**
- a **long-handled grabber** so you do not need to bend

Your council can arrange a free **needs assessment**, and an **occupational therapist** may suggest equipment and changes to your home.

## Sleep

Pain can make sleep hard. A **pillow between or under your knees** can help, as can a regular bedtime routine and taking pain relief before bed if your doctor agrees.

## Your mood

Living with long-term pain can make you feel low or anxious. This is common. Talk to your GP, or in England refer yourself to **NHS Talking Therapies**. The **Versus Arthritis helpline** (0800 5200 520) is free.
